>
>shouldn't NetHack 5.0 be lean and mean?  you know, fully featured
>and only 500k (i.e. all the fat removed).  and with portable
>profiles so you could carry around your favorite game to any NetHack
>game processor (i.e. play the same game on Unix and your favorite
>pc's, etc.).  and wouldn't it be nice if ...

No.  The dev team should make the next few versions of NetHack as
large and bloated as possible.  Eventually, this will result in the
program breaking completely under MS-DOS.  The current kludge is
brilliant, but the MS-DOS system can only take so much abuse, so the
breakage is inevitable.  With millions of users completely and
hopelessly addicted to NetHack, there will be a mass migration of
users to operating systems that can handle it in a direct manner, such
as Unix, VMS, MacOS, and even (gasp!) AmigaDos.  The users will be
happy with their NetHack fix, but will then notice that their new
operating system is missing certain ludicrous shortcomings that they
took for granted before.  As the users all type a file name with more
than eight characters before a period, they will all wake up from
their trances.  There will be a massive shout of "What the hell was I
thinking?!?"  MS-DOS will die quietly, unmourned.  Bill Gates will
feel silly and have to get married.  The drought will end in
California.  Saddam Hussein will turn out to be a nice guy, so George
Bush will have to admit that his war was a bad idea.  Gorbachev and
Yeltsin will be good buddies and fix the economy in the Soviet Union.
All of the gangs in LA will sell their weapons and donate their money
to the US government, thus solving the violence problem and the budget
deficit in one fell swoop.....

There's a lot more, but, well, you get the idea.  It would a bad idea
to reduce the size of NetHack.

>huh?  my pills?  its that time again??  so soon??? ...

Sorry, I took them all.

On a more serious note, it wouldn't be all THAT hard to reduce the
size of the executable.  A large part of the executable is text, in
the form of strings.  This is shown in how neatly the executable file
will compress in certain archive programs.  You could possibly store
these in a separate file.  This sort of data might be easier to
manipulate than a straight executable, since you wouldn't be fooling
with overlays (maybe).  This sort of data could be swapped in and out
of expanded/extended RAM in some way, and you could even keep it on
some sort of disk file, loading in messages as needed.  Of course,
this would be a lot of work....

